<p><strong>The cash gap becomes findable when every payment is recorded against its booking at the counter, by method. Closing then compares two records instead of rebuilding the day from memory.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>The 9 pm count</h2>
<p>The till is short by a few thousand rupees. The receptionist thinks a customer paid half in cash and half by card. The therapist remembers a discount. The manager was in a meeting. Everybody is tired, so the gap goes into the notebook as "difference" and the same thing happens next week.</p>
<h2>Where the gap usually hides</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Part-payments</strong> that were taken but not written down.</li><li><strong>Discounts</strong> given at the counter without a record.</li><li><strong>Mixed methods</strong> where cash, card and online are added together.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is solved</h2>
<p>With <a href="/features/payment-tracking/">payment tracking</a>, each payment is entered against its booking when it happens, with its method, including part-payments. <a href="/features/discount-management/">Discounts need approval</a>, so each has an amount and a name next to it. At night, the day's revenue by method is already there. You count the till, compare, and close the day, which locks it.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>Record every payment at the counter, not at night.</li><li>Write down the method for each one.</li><li>Do not give a discount that nobody approved.</li></ol>
